<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucania was an ancient region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, named after the Oscan-speaking Lucanians who occupied it from the 5th century BCE. Lying between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-taranto">Gulf of Taranto</a>, it included Greek cities such as Poseidonia (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paestum">Paestum</a>), Elea and Metapontum. Under Augustus it formed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruttium">Bruttium</a>, the third region of Roman Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucania.jpg" alt="Lucania" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Historical Atlas by William R. Shepherd 1911 edition File:Shepherd-c-030-031.jpg: Accurimbono derivative work: Cristi... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucania_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
